# Building Performance Output Report Specification

## Identification

| Element        | Value                                              |
| --------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| Schema Name     | `Building Performance Output Report`                |
| Schema Author   | `IBPSA-USA Building Data Exchange Committee`         |
| Schema URL      | `https://ibpsa-usa.github.io/output-reporting/`      |

## Version History

See [GitHub releases](https://github.com/IBPSA-USA/output-reporting/releases) for this
schema's version history.

## Use Case

### User Types

- **Building Owners:** The parties who own or operate the building a report describes.
- **Authorities Having Jurisdiction (AHJs):** The regulatory bodies responsible for verifying code compliance for a building.
- **Software Developers:** The engineers who build the building performance simulation, reporting, and review
    software that produces and consumes conformant documents.
- **Building Performance Modelers:** The practitioners who run building performance simulations to produce the results a
    conformant document reports.

### Simulation Software to AHJ Reporting/Review Software

The data model's core use is a software-to-software pipeline: a building performance
simulation tool exports a `BuildingPerformanceOutputReport`-conformant file, and a downstream
application -- most notably an AHJ's code-compliance review software -- ingests it to perform
automated QA/QC and compliance checks, without manual re-entry or bespoke per-tool parsing.

### Assembling a Custom Report from Multiple Software Tools

A secondary use case merges output from more than one simulation tool into a single
conformant file, when a sophisticated model (e.g., a complex mixed-use building or
design-assist study) isn't produced by one engine end to end, and a separate `TimeIntervals`
entry is needed per native timestep where the merged tools disagree on granularity (see
Publishing Rules for the `is_custom` mechanism used to absorb tool-specific breakdowns).

## Scope

This document defines the content and structure of a data model for exchanging whole-building
energy performance results produced by building performance simulation software: energy
consumption broken out by canonical end-use category and by energy source, whether that
consumption is regulated or unregulated by code, on-site energy production and export, and the
time-series structure used to report it.

All quantities reported by this data model are **site energy**: energy delivered to, or
exported from, the building at its site boundary (e.g., metered utility consumption, delivered
fuel, on-site generation), as distinct from source (primary) energy, which additionally
accounts for generation and delivery losses upstream of the site (e.g., power plant and grid
losses for electricity). This document does not define a site-to-source conversion; see
Exclusions.

This is a special case of the general data model for building data exchange published in
**ANSI/ASHRAE/IBPSA Standard 232**; where this document does not otherwise specify a structure,
convention, or data type, Standard 232 governs.

### Units

All energy quantities in this data model (consumption, on-site production, and export alike)
are reported in kilowatt-hours (kWh). This data model does not support reporting energy
quantities in any other unit.

Informative Note: A single schema-wide unit is used so that no consumer needs to parse or
convert an arbitrary per-document unit string. kWh is a common unit among many building performance
simulation tools and commonly used for energy reporting. Converting from other units is straightforward within export
tooling. kWh is an exact expression of the coherent SI unit of energy (1 kWh = 3.6 MJ, derived
directly from the definition of the second), though kWh is itself a conventional composite unit
(the SI kilo- prefix combined with the non-SI hour), not itself a coherent SI unit, and is not
coherent with power or demand units the way joules are with watts (converting kWh to kW
requires a factor of 3,600). No power or demand time series exists in this data model today, so
that distinction does not currently apply.

### Applicability

This data model is intended for the exchange of energy performance output between building
performance simulation software and downstream consumers of that output, particularly:

- Automated code-compliance review and QA/QC software, where a simulation tool exports a
  conformant file and reviewing software ingests it directly, without manual re-entry or
  bespoke per-tool parsing (see Application Rules for guidance specific to this use).
- Assembly of a single reporting file from multiple specialized building performance
  simulation tools (e.g., separate engines for whole-building loads/HVAC, daylighting, and
  on-site generation), using the `is_custom` end-use and energy-source mechanism for
  breakdowns that do not map onto the canonical categories (see Publishing Rules).
- General benchmarking, reporting, and visualization of simulated building energy performance.

### Exclusions

This document does not cover:

- Descriptions of the building or its systems (e.g., geometry, envelope constructions,
  equipment schedules, HVAC configuration) used to produce the reported results -- this data
  model reports simulation *output* only.
- Verification that the `consumption` values reported across multiple `EndUse` or
  `EnergySource` entries in the same document are non-overlapping, or that they sum to a
  correct building total. Placement of end-use energy (e.g., fan energy reported flat versus
  nested under Space Heating or Space Cooling) is a modeling judgment left to the data
  publisher; see Verification Rules and Publishing Rules.
- Conversion of reported site energy to source (primary) energy. Site-to-source factors are
  grid-mix- and jurisdiction-specific and are the responsibility of downstream applications,
  not this data model.

## Data Model

A conformant `BuildingPerformanceOutputReport` document is a single object with three
top-level members: `metadata` (the Standard 232 common `Metadata` data group, constrained to
`schema_name = "Building Performance Output Report"`), `time_intervals` (one or more
`TimeIntervals` data groups, each identified by an `id` that `TimeSeries` elsewhere in the
document reference), and `energy_sources` (one or more `EnergySource` entries -- the actual
reported results).

`EnergySource` names the energy carrier delivered to or from the site -- despite the similar
name, it is unrelated to "source energy" (primary energy) in the site-vs-source sense described
in Scope, which this data model does not report; an `EnergySource` entry reports *what* carrier
was delivered, always in site-energy terms, never *where upstream* it originated.

Each `EnergySource` (e.g., electricity, natural gas) carries a `direction` (`IMPORTED`,
`EXPORTED`, or `ON_SITE_PRODUCTION`) and an array of `EndUse` entries describing what consumed,
produced, or exported that energy. `EndUse` is recursive: any entry may carry `subcategories`,
an array of further `EndUse` entries, to any depth, alongside its own `consumption` (a
`TimeSeries` whose `time_series_type` is constrained to `SUM`, referencing one of the
document's `TimeIntervals` by `id`). An `EndUse` with subcategories may or may not also report
its own `consumption` directly, depending on whether the reported total is already implicit in
the sum of its subcategories.

### Canonical Energy Sources

An `EnergySource.name` should be one of the canonical sources below unless `is_custom` is set
to `true`. Each may be reported with any applicable `direction`; e.g., on-site photovoltaic
generation is reported as `Electricity` with `direction = ON_SITE_PRODUCTION`, not as a
separate source name.

- **Electricity** -- Electric energy supplied to or from the building via the utility grid or
  on-site generation or storage.
- **Natural Gas** -- Gaseous fuel delivered via utility pipeline.
- **Fuel Oil** -- Liquid petroleum-based fuel delivered and stored on-site.
- **Propane** -- Liquefied petroleum gas delivered and stored on-site.
- **Chilled Water** -- Cooling energy delivered as chilled water from a district or campus
  cooling system.
- **Hot Water** -- Heating energy delivered as hot water from a district or campus heating
  system.
- **Steam** -- Heating energy delivered as steam from a district or campus steam system.

### Canonical End-Use Categories

An `EndUse.name` should be one of the canonical categories below unless `is_custom` is set to
`true`. These categories and their nesting are also captured in machine-readable form as the
`Canonical End Uses` custom attribute on `EnergySource.end_uses`, in the Data Groups tables
below.

#### Lighting

Energy used by luminaires. Always reported through one of its two subcategories, never as a
bare `Lighting` value with its own consumption.

- **Interior Lighting** -- Energy used by luminaires within enclosed spaces for the purpose of
  illuminance, covering task and process lighting, dwelling- and common-area lighting,
  plug-in and hardwired fixtures, all lamp types, exit and emergency lighting, and
  decorative, public-area, and health/safety lighting.
- **Exterior Lighting** -- Energy used by luminaires that illuminate the building facade,
  entrances, walkways, parking lots, and landscaping.

#### Space Heating

Energy used by mechanical equipment (e.g., boilers, furnaces, heat pumps, radiant systems) to
add sensible heat to the building to maintain comfort or process requirements. For heat pumps,
this includes the outdoor fan's energy when its power is included in the rated efficiency.

- **Heat Pump Supplementary** -- Energy used by auxiliary or backup heating equipment (e.g.,
  electric resistance coils) that operates when a heat pump cannot meet the space heating
  load alone, or during defrost cycles.
- **Fans** -- Fan energy attributable to this system's heating operation (e.g., a furnace's
  or heat pump's supply fan), when attributing it to Space Heating better serves the report
  than the flat, top-level Fans category. See Fans below for the full definition and the
  placement convention governing whether fan energy is nested here or reported flat.

#### Space Cooling

Energy used by mechanical equipment (e.g., chillers, DX compressors) to remove sensible
and/or latent heat from the building to maintain comfort or process requirements. For DX
equipment, this includes the outdoor condenser fan's energy when its power is included in the
rated efficiency.

- **Heat Rejection** -- Energy used by central plant equipment (e.g., cooling towers, fluid
  coolers, dry coolers) to reject heat from the building's water-cooled systems to the
  outdoor environment. Excludes condenser fans integral to packaged or split DX equipment,
  whose energy stays under Space Cooling itself.
- **Fans** -- Fan energy attributable to this system's cooling operation (e.g., a packaged DX
  unit's supply fan), when attributing it to Space Cooling better serves the report than the
  flat, top-level Fans category. See Fans below.

#### Water Heating

Energy used to heat water for domestic, commercial, or industrial use (e.g., lavatories,
showers, process equipment), distinct from space heating. Also known as Service Water Heating
(SWH) or Domestic Hot Water (DHW); **Water Heating** is the name adopted here.

#### Miscellaneous

Energy used by general equipment plugged into standard electrical outlets, as well as general
hard-wired equipment not explicitly categorized in another end use.

- **Cooking** -- Energy used by commercial or residential kitchen equipment for food
  preparation that involves heating (e.g., ovens, ranges, fryers, steamers). Excludes kitchen
  exhaust fans and non-heating food processing equipment.
- **Refrigeration** -- Energy used by equipment designed to cool and store perishable goods
  below room temperature (e.g., walk-in coolers, freezers, display cases).
- **Elevators and Escalators** -- Energy used by motors, controls, and associated machinery
  for the mechanical conveyance of people and goods, including elevators, escalators, and
  moving walkways.

#### Fans

Electrical energy used by motors driving air-moving devices within the HVAC system (e.g.,
supply, return, and relief fans), as well as process or dedicated exhaust fans (e.g., kitchen
hoods, garage, or laboratory exhaust), for the purpose of circulating or exhausting air.

Fan energy may be reported either as this flat top-level category or nested as a subcategory
under Space Heating or Space Cooling, when attributing fan energy to the system it serves is
preferred -- both forms are illustrated in the `Canonical End Uses` custom attribute below.
Both are valid; see Publishing Rules and Application Rules for the convention that governs
how publishers should document, and applications should interpret, which slice of the
building's fan energy a given `Fans` entry covers.

#### Humidification

Energy used by mechanical equipment to add moisture to the building air to maintain a minimum
relative humidity setpoint.

#### Pumps

Electrical energy used by motors driving fluid-moving devices within the HVAC or service
water systems (e.g., chilled water, hot water, condenser water, recirculation, or process
water pumps).

